ORPHANED_LLAMA_SERVER=1 has been on the attention line of every hourly check this
session and I reported it each time as a live condition. It is one row logged at
00:04Z. The pid is gone, nothing listens on 8919, and the only llama-matching
process is an unrelated ollama daemon with ten days of uptime.

The check counts what it was built to count. The defect is that a per-day event
tally renders under a heading a reader parses as current state, so a resolved
midnight incident is indistinguishable from an open one at 15:13Z.

Second defect of this class in the same instrument, both mine. The head line
prints local time under a UTC header; this line prints an event count as a
condition. Neither is a wrong number. Both are numbers whose meaning does not
survive how the line is read: when a dashboard aggregates over a window, the label
must name the window or every reader assumes now.

Both fixes deferred as ONE change rather than two, because both are display
semantics in the same file and each needs a proof that bites the rendered line
rather than a helper's return value.

